For the explanation:
- Let i be the 1-indexed ranking position
- reli for the relevance of the item at i
- k for the cutoff
- R for the total number of relevant items in the answer key

Discounted Cumulative Gain at k:
sum from i = 1 to k of: reli / log2(i+1)

Each item's relevance is divided by a logarithmic discount that grows with its rank, so items deeper in the list contribute less.

This equation is ensuring relevance at the top should count more than relevance at the bottom, and the reward should decay smoothly rather than having a hard cutoff.

The log in the denominator is specifically what controls that decay. You could divide by ii
i instead, but that punishes rank 2 very harshly compared to rank 1 (half the credit immediately). Log grows slowly, so the penalty for sliding one position further down is gentle at the top and becomes even gentler as you go deeper — which matches how users actually scan a list. Rank 1 vs 2 matters more than rank 9 vs 10.

Note that we then divide by the ideal discounted cumulative gain (the decay of the songs ranked on their actual ranking)

Raw Score Rangs:
A score near 1.0 means the relevant songs landed almost exactly where a perfect model would put them — right at the top, in roughly ideal order. In practice this only happens with Set A (the control set built from feature-neighbors), and even then it won't be a perfect 1.0 because the model doesn't have access to the exact centroid you used to build that set.

A score in the 0.5–0.8 range means the model is consistently finding the relevant songs but not always ranking them first — they're present in the top k but scattered through it. This is a healthy working range for a model at this stage.A score in the 0.2–0.5 range means the relevant songs are showing up but deep in the list, or only some of them are surfacing at all. The model has some signal but it's weak.

A score near 0 means the model is essentially placing the relevant songs at the bottom of the ranking — no better than random, and possibly worse.

Based on Set A (mathmatically similar songs) and Set B (similar feeling songs):
Set A will always score higher than Set B for your current model because Set A is built from the same feature space the model uses — it's the control. The interesting reading is the size of the gap:

A large gap (say A = 0.80, B = 0.35) means the model is very good at finding what it mathematically defines as similar but those songs don't align well with what a human would group together. The math and the perception are diverging. This is your current expected state — an audio-feature-only model will have this gap.
A small gap (say A = 0.78, B = 0.68) means the model's notion of similarity is starting to track human perception closely. The features it uses are capturing something real about how people experience music.